.SH CONFIGURATION & SCHEMA SPECIFICATIONS
.SS Settings Store (bashmenu.yml)
Stores nested, dot-notation accessible key-value settings. Any nested path (e.g.
settings.dns.ipv4) can be interpolated inside menu files or actions.
The special setting
.I settings.status_gutter
defines which system badges are displayed in the bottom right corner (the status
gutter). This value is specified as a string of badges separated by pipe symbols
(|). Neither the status gutter nor the left-aligned help gutter ever exceeds
50% of the screen width. Badges and help action items are delimited by pipe
symbols (|) and treated as atomic units. When text exceeds 50% of the screen
width, overlapping items drop to the next line. Neither gutter ever exceeds two
lines, with two lines being the maximum allowed height.
Available badges include:
.I {user}, {username}
(current user),
.I {host}
(hostname),
.I {battery}
(battery percentage),
.I {date_time_12}, {date_time_24}
(full date/time),
.I {date_time_12_short}, {date_time_24_short}
(short date/time; optimal for performance),
.I {time_12}, {time_24}
(current time),
.I {time_12_short}, {time_24_short}
(short time; optimal for performance),
.I {date}
(YYYY-MM-DD), and
.I {utc_seconds}
(epoch seconds).
Status refresh frequency is automatically set to 1.0s if second-precision tokens are present, or 5.0s if minute-precision or static badges are used. Users can explicitly configure
.I settings.refresh_interval
in seconds (e.g. 1.0) or milliseconds (e.g. 1000) to override this default.
.SS Menu Structure (bashmenu.mnu)
The menu is defined as a hierarchical YAML options list. Options can utilize the
following configuration keys:
.TP
.B type
Option action type (see SECTION TYPES).
.TP
.B label
Row label. Can use dynamic placeholders.
.TP
.B icon
Icon tags utilizing brackets: {nf:[char]:[nerd-font hex]:[emoji]}.
.TP
.B action, file
Shell command, script, editor file, or notebook YAML notes path ({notebooks_dir}/notes.yml).
.TP
.B template, target, block_id
Path, destination, and unique tag for dynamic templates (inject_block).
.TP
.B quiet, stream, interactive, external, user_mode
Execution privilege and console behavior flags.
.TP
.B key, picker, start_dir, masked
Dot-notation settings key and visual configuration chooser flags.
.TP
.B show_whitespace, tab_to_spaces, tabstop
Text editor layout configurations.
.TP
.B on_yes, on_no, message
Confirmation options and alert prompts.
.SH SECTION TYPES
.TP
.B submenu
Opens a nested submenu options list.
.TP
.B command
Executes a standard shell command.
.TP
.B script
Launches an external script or executable.
.TP
.B config
Prompts user to edit setting value.
.TP
.B toggle
True/False/Cancel settings toggle modal.
.TP
.B radio
Select single setting value from radio button list modal.
.TP
.B editor
Opens file in built-in Nano-style editor.
.TP
.B notebook
Opens dynamic notes collection menu from YAML file ({notebooks_dir}/notes.yml).
Supports interactive task-list checkboxes, Markdown/BBCode modal viewers, full TextArea editor modals with smart list auto-formatting, ghost text, shared cross-interface clipboard ([C]/Ctrl+C/Ctrl+V), and help gutter note creation ([N]).
.TP
.B confirm
Launches Yes/No confirmation dialog box.
.TP
.B message
Informational alert or popup dialog.
.TP
.B python
Dispatches internal Python routine.
.TP
.B inject_block
Installs or uninstalls template blocks inside profiles.
.TP
.B theme_selector
Launches list of registered color themes.
.TP
.B toast
Triggers a non-blocking, self-dismissing toast notification docked in the bottom-right corner above the status gutter (supports message, title, and seconds/timeout).
.TP
.B {divider}
Renders an aesthetic non-selectable horizontal line separator styled by the active theme.
.TP
.B back
Navigates back to the preceding submenu level.
.TP
.B exit
Shuts down the menu interface completely.
.SH DYNAMIC DIRECTIVES & SYSTEM PLACEHOLDERS
.SS Dynamic Input Directives
These directives prompt the user inside modal dialogs, replacing the placeholder
with the chosen value prior to action execution.
.TP
.B {param}
Prompts for a single-line text value using a modal input box. Supports
\fBtitle\fR (modal header), \fBprompt\fR (body prompt text), and
\fBmasked\fR (boolean to mask input with asterisks for secrets/passwords).
.TP
.B {file_picker}
Launches a visual file chooser dialog box.
.TP
.B {file_picker:path:<dir>}
Launches a visual file chooser dialog box defaulting to the specified directory.
.TP
.B {file_picker_new}, {file_picker:new}
Launches a visual file chooser dialog box allowing creation of new files.
.TP
.B {dir_picker}
Launches a visual directory chooser dialog box.
.TP
.B {dir_picker:path:<dir>}
Launches a visual directory chooser dialog box defaulting to the specified directory.
.TP
.B {dir_picker_new}, {dir_picker:new}
Launches a visual directory chooser dialog box allowing creation of new folders.
.SS Path Modifiers for Pickers
Pickers support suffix modifiers to extract subcomponents of the chosen path prior to shell execution:
.TP
.B {file_picker:stem}
Full path without the file extension (e.g. "/path/to/img" from "/path/to/img.png").
.TP
.B {file_picker:name}
Filename with extension (e.g. "img.png").
.TP
.B {file_picker:base}
Filename without extension (e.g. "img").
.TP
.B {file_picker:dir}
Parent directory path (e.g. "/path/to").
.TP
.B {file_picker:ext}
File extension including leading dot (e.g. ".png").
.TP
.B {dir_picker:name}
Folder name without parent path.
.TP
.B {dir_picker:dir}
Parent directory path of the chosen directory.
.SS Picker Auto-Starting Folders
Default starting folders can be configured inline using \fB:path:<dir>\fR (e.g.
"{file_picker:path:{bashmenu_dir}/assets/wallpapers}"), or by preceding
the placeholder with a folder path (e.g. "{templates_dir}/{file_picker}" or
"~/projects/{dir_picker}"). Both methods set the visual chooser's start
folder and avoid double-prefixing.
.SS System Placeholders
The following placeholders are auto-interpolated inside actions or templates:
.TP
.B {user}, {username}
Current system username.
.TP
.B {host}, {hostname}
System host name.
.TP
.B {user-mode}
Current privilege mode ("root" if running with root/sudo privileges, otherwise "user").
.TP
.B {version}
Script version (e.g., 0.0.1).
.TP
.B {home}
User's absolute home directory path.
.TP
.B {bashmenu_dir}
User configuration root directory (~/.config/bashmenu).
.TP
.B {dev_dir}, {repo_dir}
Local git repository and development workspace root directory.
.TP
.B {templates_dir}, {templates}, {scripts_dir}, {scripts}
Templates and scripts folders paths.
.TP
.B {cache_dir}, {cache}
Cache folder paths (defaults to ~/.cache/bashmenu). Sets $CACHE_DIR.
.TP
.B {notebooks_dir}, {notebooks}
Custom notes and notebooks directory path ({bashmenu_dir}/notebooks).
.TP
.B {bashrc}, {zshrc}, {profile}, {bash_profile}, {zprofile}, {shell_profile}
User shell profile configuration paths with intelligent existence fallback.
.TP
.B {vimrc}, {nanorc}, {bash_aliases}
Editor configuration files and shell alias paths.
.TP
.B {prefix}, {termux_properties}, {termux_storage}
Termux environment prefix and mobile terminal paths.
.TP
.B {powershell_profile}, {appdata}, {userprofile}
Windows / PowerShell profile scripts and environment paths.
.TP
.B {divider}
Themed horizontal divider line (defaults to {ascii:196} across window width).
.TP
.B {date_time_12}, {date_time_24}
12-hour and 24-hour formatted date-time (includes seconds).
.TP
.B {date_time_12_short}, {date_time_24_short}
Short 12-hour and 24-hour formatted date-time (excludes seconds; optimal for performance).
.TP
.B {date}
Current date (YYYY-MM-DD).
.TP
.B {time_12}, {time_24}
12-hour and 24-hour current time (includes seconds).
.TP
.B {time_12_short}, {time_24_short}
Short 12-hour and 24-hour current time (excludes seconds; optimal for performance).
.TP
.B {battery}
Current battery percentage (e.g. 84% or N/A). Includes a 5-second
performance cache.
.TP
.B {battery_stat}, {battery_status}
Current battery charging status (e.g. Charging, Discharging, Full, or N/A). Includes a 5-second
performance cache.
.TP
.B {theme}
Active color theme name from bashmenu.yml (e.g. tokyo_night, dracula).
.TP
.B {localip}
Primary outbound IPv4 address (e.g. 192.168.1.50). Evaluated on demand
via UDP socket probe with TTL cache.
.TP
.B {utc_seconds}
Current UTC epoch seconds.
.TP
.B {window_width}, {window_height}
Current active window width (columns) and height (lines) in characters.
.TP
.B {ascii:decimal}
Prints character corresponding to decimal code (uses CP437 for extended ASCII, e.g. {ascii:168} resolves to ¿).
.TP
.B {app.<key.path>}, {<key.path>}, {settings.dot_key}, {user.dot_key}
Configuration key lookup from bashmenu.yml using explicit app namespace (e.g.,
{app.theme}, {app.settings.tabstop}, {app.user.postal_code}) or shorthand format
({settings.cache_dir}, {user.editor}, {user.divider}).
.TP
.B {command:shell_cmd}
Dynamic shell command execution placeholder. Runs shell_cmd via the system shell, trims trailing whitespace/newlines, and replaces the tag with the command output. Fully supports nested curly braces (e.g., {command:hostname -I | awk '{print $1}'}). Features a 30.0-second performance cache TTL and a 3.0-second safety execution timeout.
.TP
.B {toast:SECONDS:"Message"}, {toast:"Message"}
Configurable non-blocking toast notification directive. Displays a floating toast notification on the bottom-right corner for the specified duration in seconds (default 2.0s) and resolves to an empty string.
.SS Nerd Fonts & Emoji Adaptive Resolution
The application resolves icons dynamically using three properties: a fallback
ASCII character, a Nerd Font PUA code point, and a Unicode emoji icon:
.RS
.B {nf:[char]:[nerd-font hex]:[emoji-glyph]}
.RE
Precedence is evaluated as follows:
.IP \(bu 2
.B Emoji Icon (Unicode capable)
\- Chosen if system is Unicode capable, use_nerd_fonts is enabled, and emoji is provided.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Nerd Font Glyph (Nerd Fonts enabled)
\- Chosen if system is Unicode capable, use_nerd_fonts is true, and hex is
provided. Translates and renders the Nerd Font glyph.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Fallback Character
\- Displayed if Nerd Fonts are disabled or no icon/hex is defined (e.g., {nf:#} displays '#').
.IP \(bu 2
.B Fallback
\- Resolves to empty string if all properties are unconfigured.
.PP
Every menu option icon slot occupies a standardized 4-column display width (vis_w + pad_w = 4), ensuring that all menu labels and descriptions strictly align vertically at column 11 regardless of icon presence or type. Unicode variation selectors are normalized to ensure terminal font cell alignment.
.SS Console Tag Rich Formatting
The application incorporates a rich inline formatting parser allowing developers to
use nested bracketed formatting tags inside labels, headers, status gutters, and dialogues.
.TP
.B [b]text[/b]
Renders bold text (Rich Style(bold=True)).
.TP
.B [u]text[/u]
Renders underlined text (Rich Style(underline=True)).
.TP
.B [dim]text[/dim]
Renders dimmed contrast text (Rich Style(dim=True)).
.TP
.B [reverse]text[/reverse]
Renders reversed contrast text (Rich Style(reverse=True)).
.TP
.B [color=color_name]text[/color]
Renders custom theme-colored text. Supports active theme semantic tokens (text, border, title, highlight, footer, help_text, plugin, shortcut_key, accent, divider, background, selection, gutter, button_primary, button_error, button_cancel, button_success, window_close_button, scrollbar, whitespace), named ANSI/bright constants (COLOR_BLACK, COLOR_RED, COLOR_GREEN, COLOR_YELLOW, COLOR_BLUE, COLOR_MAGENTA, COLOR_CYAN, COLOR_WHITE, COLOR_GREY, COLOR_GRAY, COLOR_BRIGHT_BLACK, COLOR_BRIGHT_RED, COLOR_BRIGHT_GREEN, COLOR_BRIGHT_YELLOW, COLOR_BRIGHT_BLUE, COLOR_BRIGHT_MAGENTA, COLOR_BRIGHT_CYAN, COLOR_BRIGHT_WHITE, COLOR_ORANGE, COLOR_PURPLE, COLOR_PINK, COLOR_BROWN), 256-color palette numbers (0 to 255), and 24-bit TrueColor hex values (#rrggbb).
.TP
.B [table width=100%][tr][th]Col[/th][/tr][tr][td]Val[/td][/tr][/table]
Renders an aligned box-drawing Unicode table with dynamically fitted or percentage-based column widths (e.g. 50/50, 33/33/33), bold headers, in-cell word wrapping, and optional row separators.
.TP
.B [list][*]Item 1[*]Item 2[/list]
Renders an unordered bulleted list (using bullet glyphs). Closing [/*] tags are optional.
.TP
.B [list=1][*]Item 1[*]Item 2[/list]
Renders an ordered numbered list (1., 2., etc.). Also supports alphabetical lists ([list=a]).
.PP
.B Formatting Rules & Feature Flags:
.IP \(bu 2
.B Code Block & Backtick Suppression:
Formatting tags within code backticks (`[b]code[/b]`) or fenced code blocks are automatically suppressed and rendered literally.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Backslash Tag Escaping:
Preceding a tag bracket with a backslash (e.g. \[rs][b] or \[rs][/b]) escapes the parser, outputting literal bracketed tag text.
.IP \(bu 2
.B no_formatting Directive:
Modals and text formatting conversion utilities accept an optional no_formatting parameter to disable tag processing entirely.
.SS Color Themes Specification (bashmenu.themes)
Color themes configure the visual color scheme for the main menu, visual menu editor, text editor, and modal screens. Themes are defined under top-level theme names (dracula, nord, cyberpunk, gruvbox, qbasic, pacman, industry, matrix, monochrome, synthwave, amber_crt, hotdog_stand) for 256, 16, and 8 color modes using single-line compact lists [fg, bg].
.PP
Supported Theme Elements: background, title, border, text, highlight, accent,
footer, help_text (configures help text in bottom gutter and modal footers),
plugin (configures text color of background plugin feeds), gutter, selection,
shortcut_key, divider, button_primary, button_error,
button_cancel, button_success, window_close_button, scrollbar, and whitespace (configures
non-visible whitespace character color in the text editor).
.PP
.B Formatting Rule for Editor Compatibility:
Theme definitions maintain compact single-line flow-style bracket lists (e.g. title: [201, -1]). This enables bashedit.py's --display-theme-colors feature to accurately parse inline bracketed color values and render live color swatch previews.
.PP
.B Window Border & Divider Customization (window: and divider:):
Themes can optionally customize outer window frame border characters using a
top-level \fBwindow:\fR section, and horizontal dividers using a top-level
\fBdivider:\fR section. Authors can specify CP437 ASCII directives (e.g.,
{ascii:205}, {ascii:221}), Unicode characters, or Nerd Font glyphs. Supported
window border keys: \fBborder_horizontal_top\fR, \fBborder_horizontal_bottom\fR,
\fBborder_vertical_left\fR, \fBborder_vertical_right\fR (with \fBborder_horizontal\fR
and \fBborder_vertical\fR as shorthands), \fBborder_top_left\fR,
\fBborder_top_right\fR, \fBborder_bottom_left\fR, \fBborder_bottom_right\fR,
\fBborder_tee_top\fR / \fBtop_tee\fR, \fBborder_tee_bottom\fR / \fBbottom_tee\fR,
\fBborder_tee_left\fR / \fBleft_tee\fR, \fBborder_tee_right\fR / \fBright_tee\fR,
\fBborder_cross\fR, \fBgutter_border\fR, \fBgutter_separator\fR, \fBgutter_left_tee\fR,
\fBgutter_right_tee\fR, \fBgutter_top_tee\fR, \fBgutter_bottom_tee\fR,
\fBtitle_left_cap\fR, \fBtitle_right_cap\fR, and \fBshadow_char\fR.
Omitted keys fall back to standard single-line box drawing characters.
Dividers strictly follow a 4-tier style priority: theme divider definitions
override plugin divider definitions in bashmenu.yml, which override declared
definitions in bashmenu.mnu, which fall back to {ascii:196}. Outer window
borders never override dividers. Dividers only appear in a menu if declared as
an option in bashmenu.mnu. Dividers of length \fB{window_width}\fR render
within standard margins with vertical borders and no tees; dividers of length
\fB{screen_width}\fR run border to border using left and right tees without margins.
.SS Wallpaper Backgrounds & TrueColor Compositing (settings.background)
HA Bash Menu features a TrueColor half-block (\(bu) wallpaper compositing engine that renders graphical background images directly behind menus, options, and status lines using universal ANSI text capabilities.
Wallpapers can be specified in user settings (\fIsettings.background\fR in bashmenu.yml) or bundled with themes (\fItheme_background\fR in bashmenu.themes).
Supported settings:
.TP
.B enabled
Boolean master switch enabling or disabling wallpaper compositing.
.TP
.B image
Path to wallpaper image (PNG or JPG format). Supports system placeholders like {bashmenu_dir}.
.TP
.B opacity
Float factor between 0.0 (transparent) and 1.0 (opaque), default 0.35. Lower values blend more theme background tint.
.TP
.B scaling
Image scaling mode: "cover" (maintains aspect ratio, auto-cropping), "stretch" (exact fit), "fit" (proportional inside window), "center" (1:1 original size), or "tile" (pattern repeat).
.TP
.B clustering
Integer delta threshold between 0 and 128 (default: 0). Default 0 preserves full photographic fidelity and gradients without horizontal run-length streaking. Values above 0 merge neighboring spans for high-latency connections.
.TP
.B quantization
Color channel quantization step between 1 and 32 (default: 1). Default 1 retains full 24-bit TrueColor depth without banding.
.SH MODULARITY & PLUGINS
.SS Dynamic Block Injection
The inject_block type installs templates inside profile files (e.g. .bashrc).
It wraps the template in markers (# CODEBLOCK:block_id:START/END) to manage
seamless interactive installs, updates, and uninstalls.
.SS In-Process Python Plugins (external: false)
Python script items of type "script" can set "external: false" to run inside
bashmenu's parent process, avoiding terminal flashes. Plugins must implement
a Textual Screen subclass pushed via screen.app.push_screen().
.SS Background Footer Plugins & Plugin Creation
HA Bash Menu features an asynchronous, background-cached plugin architecture configured under
.I settings.plugins
in
.I bashmenu.yml.
Plugin output lines are rendered in a dedicated display area above the help footer.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Asynchronous Execution & Caching:
Plugin scripts run in background daemon threads with re-entrant locking to ensure network calls never lag the main TUI rendering loop. Script outputs are cached in memory for the duration specified by
.I sleep
(in seconds, defaulting to 300). Setting
.I sleep: 0
forces live execution on every frame refresh.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Priority Rendering:
Menu options hold top rendering priority. The engine calculates available vertical screen space between the menu options list and help footer; if terminal height is constrained, plugin lines are automatically wrapped, truncated, or dropped so menu navigation remains unhindered.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Pretext & Posttext:
Plugins support optional
.I pretext
and
.I posttext
lines (such as {user.divider}) rendered before and after script output lines.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Declarative Multi-Column Table Layout & Entries Chunking:
Plugins can be arranged horizontally in equal-width columns (e.g. 50/50, 33/33/33) across the terminal window by specifying
.I type: table
directly under
.I settings.plugins
or within a nested
.I layout
mapping. Setting
.I divider: true
(or
.I top_divider: true\c
) renders a native horizontal divider above the plugin dashboard, automatically allocating vertical line budget. The
.I entries
setting (default: 2) sets the maximum plugins per table row. When additional plugins are encountered, subsequent tables are constructed automatically. Long text within cells wraps cleanly at word boundaries.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Standalone Plugins (standalone: true or span: full):
Full-width plugins such as Quote of the Day or system status banners can stretch across the entire screen on their own line without table borders by setting
.I standalone: true
or
.I span: full,
rendering cleanly above or below multi-column tables.
.PP
.B Creating & Registering a Custom Plugin:
To create a custom plugin, write an executable script in
.I scripts_dir
(e.g., scripts/my_status.sh) that outputs text to standard stdout (supporting BBCode styling tags like [b] or [color=accent]). Then register it under
.I settings.plugins
in
.I bashmenu.yml:
.RS
.nf
settings:
  plugins:
    my_status:
      script: my_status.sh
      sleep: 60
      pretext: '{user.divider}'
      posttext: '{user.divider}'
.fi
.RE
.SS Inactivity Timeout & Screensaver (settings.inactivity_timeout)
HA Bash Menu supports automated screensaver and command execution when no
keyboard or mouse interaction occurs for a specified duration. This subsystem
is configured under \fIsettings.inactivity_timeout\fR in \fIbashmenu.yml\fR:
.RS
.nf
settings:
  inactivity_timeout:
    milliseconds: 300000
    # command: 'cmatrix'
.fi
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
.B Unconfigured Default:
The timeout directive does not include a command argument by default. If this
setting is not configured, the timeout function does nothing.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Keystroke & Mouse Event Reset:
Any keyboard keystroke, mouse movement, click, or scroll resets the countdown
timer back to the start.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Alternate Screen Buffer Isolation:
When triggered, the command executes in an alternate screen buffer (\(rsx1b[?1049h)
after suspending Textual. This prevents disturbance to underlying terminal
lines and the active menu layout.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Automatic Restoration:
When the screensaver command terminates, the menu interface is seamlessly
restored and the inactivity timer is automatically re-armed.
.SS Multi-Platform Support & Environment Detection
HA Bash Menu incorporates an environment detection engine in bashmenu.sh enabling graceful degradation and multi-platform compatibility without manual configuration. All files leverage universal shebangs. Cross-Platform package wrappers automatically translate dependency installs to the correct local package manager (apt, dnf, pacman, pkg, or brew), and smartly add or omit sudo depending on whether it is running in standard Linux, macOS, WSL, or containerized user-spaces like Termux.
